title: Slow log
kind: overview
aliases: slow slow-log slow-operation-log
summary: Every repository keeps a log of the operations that took too long, with where the time went.

Some operations occasionally take seconds when they usually take milliseconds —
a query, a field write, a flush of the watcher's buffered events — and by the time
you notice, nothing recorded why. So every repository keeps a ''slow-operation
log'': one entry per operation that took longer than a threshold (2 s, or 100 ms
for one that only read), or that read an unusually large number of keys.

An entry records ''phases, not just a total'': not that a query was slow, but that
it waited three seconds for the repository a watcher flush was holding, then
spent the rest evaluating. It is on by default and kept on disk, so that a
slowdown that shows up once a day is still there when you look.

It is not a profiler or a metrics system: no aggregates, no percentiles, no
sampling of fast operations — only what crossed a threshold.

